Q: PHP foreach to make query A: <p>try this</p>
<pre><code>$arr = array(
    &quot;[12313]&quot; =&gt; &quot;1&quot;,
    &quot;[12312]&quot; =&gt; &quot;2&quot;
);
foreach ($arr as $key =&gt; $value) {
    $query = &quot;SELECT COUNT(*) as amount FROM warehouse where item='&quot; . $key . &quot;'&quot;;
    $check = $query-&gt;row_array();
    $amt = $check['amount'];
    if ($amt &gt; 0) {
        return true;
    } else {
        return false;
    }
}
</code></pre>

Q: ASP.NET webforms Ajax to pass data from aspx to code behind A: <p>Open up the RouteConfig.cs in the App_Start folder, and change this:</p>
<pre><code>        public static void RegisterRoutes(RouteCollection routes)
        {
            var settings = new FriendlyUrlSettings();
            settings.AutoRedirectMode = RedirectMode.Permanent;
            routes.EnableFriendlyUrls(settings);
        }

</code></pre>
<p>to</p>
<pre><code>        public static void RegisterRoutes(RouteCollection routes)
        {
            var settings = new FriendlyUrlSettings();
            settings.AutoRedirectMode = RedirectMode.Off;  //&lt;------ this change
            routes.EnableFriendlyUrls(settings);
        }

</code></pre>
<p>By default, most of the webforms templates start out with so called &quot;Friendly URL's&quot;, and that will cause the jQuery .ajax calls to fail.  The above change will not effect your typical web application in any meaningful way
(it add's a &quot;/&quot; to the end of friendly URL's - you don't care about this).</p>

Q: Are anonymous structures/unions compatible with their &quot;flattened&quot; form in C17? A: <p>The intent of the standard cannot be to make a structure compatible with its flattened form because that would break the language. Consider these structures:</p>
<pre><code>struct s { char x; char y; int z; };
struct s { char x; struct { char y; int z; }; };
</code></pre>
<p>In a typical C implementation with four-byte <code>int</code> with four-byte alignment requirement, there would be two bytes of padding in the first structure between <code>y</code> and <code>z</code>, putting <code>z</code> at offset 4. In the second structure, the internal structure needs to have four-byte alignment so that its <code>int</code> can be properly aligned, and it has three bytes of padding between its <code>y</code> and <code>z</code>. Then the outer structure has three bytes of padding between <code>x</code> and the internal structure, putting <code>z</code> at offset 8. Treating these two structures as compatible would not work.</p>

