Q: Round the time to either 30 or 60 minute mark (half-past current hour, or at the next hour), in Java A: <p>We can define constant <code>ALMOST_HALF_HOUR</code> and custom temporal units enum with <code>HALF_HOURS</code>:</p>
<pre class="lang-java prettyprint-override"><code>static final Duration ALMOST_HALF_HOUR = Duration.ofMinutes(30).minusNanos(1);

public enum CustomUnit implements TemporalUnit {

    HALF_HOURS(Duration.ofSeconds(1800)), // &lt;--
    DAYS(Duration.ofSeconds(86400)),
    FOREVER(Duration.ofSeconds(Long.MAX_VALUE, 999_999_999));

    private final Duration duration;

    private CustomUnit(Duration estimatedDuration) {
        this.duration = estimatedDuration;
    }

    @Override
    public Duration getDuration() { return duration; }
    @Override
    public boolean isDurationEstimated() { return this.compareTo(DAYS) &gt;= 0; }
    @Override
    public boolean isDateBased() {
        return this.compareTo(DAYS) &gt;= 0 &amp;&amp; this != FOREVER;
    }
    @Override
    public boolean isTimeBased() { return this.compareTo(DAYS) &lt; 0; }
    @SuppressWarnings(&quot;unchecked&quot;)
    @Override
    public &lt;R extends Temporal&gt; R addTo(R temporal, long amount) {
        return (R) temporal.plus(amount, this); 
    }
    @Override
    public long between(Temporal temporal1Inclusive, Temporal temporal2Exclusive) {
        return temporal1Inclusive.until(temporal2Exclusive, this); 
    }    
}
</code></pre>
<p>Then the necessary rounding can be done like this:</p>
<pre class="lang-java prettyprint-override"><code>var roundedDt = dt
    .plus(ALMOST_HALF_HOUR)
    .truncatedTo(CustomUnit.HALF_HOURS);
</code></pre>
<p>By the way, this works great when switching from winter time to summer time and back. Here is an example:</p>
<pre class="lang-java prettyprint-override"><code>
var tz = ZoneId.of(&quot;Europe/Athens&quot;);
var winterToWinterDt = ZonedDateTime.of(2026,  3, 29, 2, 20, 0, 0, tz);
var winterToSummerDt = ZonedDateTime.of(2026,  3, 29, 2, 50, 0, 0, tz);
var summerToSummerDt = ZonedDateTime.of(2026, 10, 25, 3, 20, 0, 0, tz);
var summerToWinterDt = ZonedDateTime.of(2026, 10, 25, 3, 50, 0, 0, tz);

System.out.printf(&quot;%s -&gt; %s\n%s -&gt; %s\n\n%s -&gt; %s\n%s -&gt; %s&quot;,
    winterToWinterDt, winterToWinterDt.plus(ALMOST_HALF_HOUR).truncatedTo(CustomUnit.HALF_HOURS),
    winterToSummerDt, winterToSummerDt.plus(ALMOST_HALF_HOUR).truncatedTo(CustomUnit.HALF_HOURS),
    summerToSummerDt, summerToSummerDt.plus(ALMOST_HALF_HOUR).truncatedTo(CustomUnit.HALF_HOURS),
    summerToWinterDt, summerToWinterDt.plus(ALMOST_HALF_HOUR).truncatedTo(CustomUnit.HALF_HOURS));
</code></pre>
<p>Result:</p>
<pre class="lang-none prettyprint-override"><code>2026-03-29T02:20+02:00[Europe/Athens] -&gt; 2026-03-29T02:30+02:00[Europe/Athens]
2026-03-29T02:50+02:00[Europe/Athens] -&gt; 2026-03-29T04:00+03:00[Europe/Athens]

2026-10-25T03:20+03:00[Europe/Athens] -&gt; 2026-10-25T03:30+03:00[Europe/Athens]
2026-10-25T03:50+03:00[Europe/Athens] -&gt; 2026-10-25T03:00+02:00[Europe/Athens]
</code></pre>

Q: CS50 caesar doesn&#39;t exit after non-numeric input A: <p>I am not sure if your sample code block was not formatted properly when copied into this issue, but the statement block to be executed that is associated with a for loop should be indented.  Depending upon the compiler flag settings, it might complain or warn about this setup.  Regardless, for this type of scenario, the following refactored code would offer more clarity as to what code is to be executed with the for loop.</p>
<pre><code>int main(int argc, char * argv[])
{
    if (argc != 2)
    {
        printf(&quot;Usage: %s key\n&quot;, argv[0]);
        return 1;
    }

    for (int i = 0, n = strlen(argv[1]); i &lt; n; i++)
    {
        if(isalpha(argv[1][i]))
        {
            printf(&quot;Usage: %s numeric_key\n&quot;, argv[0]);
            return 2;
        }
    }

    printf(&quot;Looked okay\n&quot;);

    return 0;
}
</code></pre>
<p>FYI, since a minimal reproducible block of code was not included, some artistic license was used to produce a very simple main function to execute the validation.  With that, following is some terminal output.</p>
<pre><code>craig@Vera:~/C_Programs/Console/Caesar_6/bin/Release$ ./Caesar_6
Usage: ./Caesar_6 key
craig@Vera:~/C_Programs/Console/Caesar_6/bin/Release$ ./Caesar_6 aa
Usage: ./Caesar_6 numeric_key
craig@Vera:~/C_Programs/Console/Caesar_6/bin/Release$ ./Caesar_6 15
Looked okay
</code></pre>
<p>While examining this code, one should note that there is still a caveat with the validation routine.  Though the validation test does screen for alphabetical characters to alert of invalid conditions, really, what should be validated is that only digits are entered as the key value.  Following is illustration of that caveat using a key value having an underscore.</p>
<pre><code>craig@Vera:~/C_Programs/Console/Caesar_6/bin/Release$ ./Caesar_6 _2
Looked okay
</code></pre>
<p>With that, one further bit of refactoring was done to refine the character test to ensure only digits are entered using the &quot;isdigit&quot; test in lieu of the &quot;isalpha&quot; test.</p>
<pre><code>    if(!(isdigit(argv[1][i])))  /* If not a digit  */
</code></pre>
<p>Thus, the previous mixed character key value would not pass.</p>
<pre><code>craig@Vera:~/C_Programs/Console/Caesar_6/bin/Release$ ./Caesar_6 _2
Usage: ./Caesar_6 numeric_key
</code></pre>
<p>So the takeaways from this issue would be further acquaint oneself with for loop usage, proper indentation, proper usage of braces to clarify blocks of code, and character testing functionality.</p>

Q: ASP.NET mobile page not redirecting for customErrors A: <p>You're using <code>redirectMode=&quot;ResponseRewrite&quot;</code>, which, unlike a regular redirect, tries to do a <code>Server.Transfer</code> to keep the URL the same.</p>
<p>If an error happens in a sub directory a relative path in <code>defaultRedirect=&quot;/Error/Error.aspx&quot;</code> might end up being resolved incorrectly or the <code>Rewrite</code> might fail because of directory depth.</p>
<p>Try changing <code>redirectMode</code> to <code>ResponseRedirect</code> temporarily. If the redirect immediately starts working, the issue is the way the server is trying to rewrite the path.</p>
<p>Additionally, in your logs, there is:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Method: LogAsync.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>If your <code>btn_submit_Click</code> is <code>async void</code> or calling async incorrectly, it could escape the standard webforms cycle. <code>customErrors</code> depends on the synchronous page lifecycle. If an exception gets thrown on a background thread the <code>Global.asax</code> will log it maybe, but the response has already been broken, and <code>customErrors</code> won't know how to handle the redirect.</p>
<p>Since your <code>Global_asax</code> is successfully catching the error in the logs, the best workaround is to manually force the redirect. Modify your <code>Application_error</code> in <code>Global.asax.cs</code> to this:</p>
<pre><code>LogAsync(&quot;Global_asax - Unhandled Exception&quot;, exc);

if (Context.IsCustomErrorEnabled)
{
    Server.ClearError(); 
    Response.Redirect(&quot;~/Error/Error.aspx&quot;);
}
</code></pre>
<p>The reason it isn't redirecting now is that in your logs, <code>Global.asax</code> handles the error, but the execution continues on to the ASP.NET handler because <code>Server.ClearError()</code> never got called.</p>
